**user**:
> I'm trying to bring results together from two models, one which uses MER prices and the other which assumes PPP everywhere. In one case, I want to assume that prices for one thing (technology costs) are the same everywhere because they have to be bought on the international market, but elsewhere I want to assume different prices (e.g. healthcare costs). How can I do this?
**assistant**:
> To address the challenge of integrating results from two models with differing price assumptions—one using Market Exchange Rates (MER) and the other using Purchasing Power Parity (PPP)—you can use the following approach:
> 
> 1. **Segmentation of Cost Components**:
>    - Separate cost components that should remain constant across regions, like technology costs, from those that should vary based on local economic conditions, such as healthcare costs.
> 
> 2. **Using MER for Internationally Traded Goods**:
>    - For items like technology costs that are consistent globally due to international market dynamics, use MER. This ensures that the prices reflect the actual exchange rates used in international trade.
> 
> 3. **Applying PPP for Local Services**:
>    - For components with localized pricing that are influenced by domestic purchasing power, apply PPP. This is ideal for services like healthcare, where local economic conditions and incomes affect costs.
> 
> 4. **Integration Strategy**:
>    - Establish a conversion framework, where you can switch between MER and PPP based on the regional nature of each cost component.
>    - For regions or sectors where both pricing approaches might apply, consider a hybrid weighting method, which takes into account the proportion of goods and services influenced by international versus local market factors.
> 
> 5. **Constructing Conversion Multipliers**:
>    - Develop conversion multipliers for each region for converting costs from MER to PPP and vice versa. This requires a detailed analysis of both economic conditions and exchange rate dynamics.
> 
> 6. **Data Consistency and Calibration**:
>    - Ensure that your datasets (for MER and PPP) are harmonized and reconciled to reflect the most recent economic data.
>    - Calibrate your models for baseline accuracy. Given the differences in scopes of MER and PPP, periodic recalibrations with updated data will be crucial.
> 
> 7. **Scenario Analysis for Validation**:
>    - Analyze scenarios where technological advancements or resource shifts change the balance of prices between international and local provision. This will not only validate your model but also simulate possible future economic conditions.
> 
> By carefully delineating international versus local pricing and utilizing appropriate conversion strategies, you'll be able to effectively integrate the results of these models. Make sure to clearly document your assumptions and methodologies to ensure transparency and replicability of your approach.
